/*!

@page Post_processing Post-processing pipe

## Overview ##

Post-processing is used for applying additional processing. Additional
processing modules are implemented as plugins. QMMF provides multiple plugins,
but customer could implement own plugins. Post-processing could be applied on
both video tracks and image. One instance of plugin could be applied on only one
track or image. If the same plugin should be applied on multiple tracks,
multiple instances should be created.

___

## Post-Processing Pipe ##

@image html /Images/RostProcPipeSimple.png

___

## Post-Processing Pipe Call Flow ##

@image html /Images/PostProcCallFlow.png
___


## Interfaces ##

There are 2 interface classes __PostProcPipe__ and __PostProcFactory__.
+   PostProcFactory is responsible to expose list of supported plugins, create,
destroy and configure of the plugin. Create plugin API returns unique ID which
is used for configuration destroy and passing post-processing pipe.
+   PostProcPipe is used for creation of plugins. It takes as argument list of
plugin IDs.

## Internal design ##

Post-processing consists of pipe manager and nodes. Pipe manager is used
for creation, linking, configuring and starting of pipe. Once nodes are linked
and started, processing is handled entirely by nodes. Nodes implement buffer
management and communication with previous and next nodes. Nodes are linked by
consumer and producer interface. The same interface is used as input and output
of the pipe. Input of the first node is input of the pipe. Output of the last
node is output of the pipe. Each node has input buffer queue, input handler and
output handler. Input queue is used for buffering input frames. Input handler is
running in own thread. This handler is responsible for waiting for input and
output buffer, and also processing start. Processing is automatically started
when buffers are available. When process is done input buffers are returned
immediately. Output buffer is sent to output handler. Output handler is running
is separate thread. It is supposed to send the buffer to the next node. Sending
the buffer from different thread guarantees that processing will not be blocked
by next node. Each plugin is handled by node. But there are internal nodes which
are not plugins. They are not exposed. Those nodes are used for format and
dimension change. Internal nodes are inserted automatically if needed. For
example if we have plugins which process RAW and YUV data. In this case we need
extra node to process RAW data to YUV. YUV to JPEG encode is also supported.
Down scale is available also in some scenarios.

## Creation of pipe ##

Create pipe API takes list of plugin IDs and output requirement. Pipe manager is
going to link plugins in the same order they are presented in the list. Pipe
manager starts from the last plugin. It sends output requirement to plugin and
query for input requirements. Input requirements are used for output requirements
of previous node. If previous node does not support such output,
Post-processing pipe automatically insert internal node. Internal node is
supposed to handle difference between output and input. If plugins output and
input differences cannot be handled by internal node, creation of pipe fails.
This process continuous until first plugin. Input requirements of first plugin
are exposed as input requirements of the pipe. Sometimes plugins support
multiple inputs for given output. Because of that, there is optional argument
of create pipe API which could provide input requirements too.

## Adding consumer producer ##

Adding new consumer triggers linking of pipe. New consumer is added to first
node. Then the first node producer is added as consumer of next node.
This process continues until last node. When GetProducer post-processing
API is called, the producer of last node is returned.

## Starting of pipe ##

During start of pipe input and output handlers are created. Plugin start API is
also called. After this point of time, pipe is ready to handle input buffers.

## Stopping the pipe ##

Stop pipe API is blocking call. It returns when all node processing is done and
all buffers are returned. If new input buffer arrived while stopping, buffer
will be returned immediately. Plugin stop API is also called.

## Aborting the pipe ##

This API could be used to speed up stopping of pipe.
It is blocking API and returns when processing of all nodes is aborted.
If new input buffer arrives while/or after abort, the buffer will be returned
immediately. This API should be always used with combination of stop API.
Processing cannot be resumed after abort API.

## Destroy pipe ##

Destroy pipe deletes all nodes. After this point of time, plugins still exist.
Plugins should be destroyed by factory class (__PostProcFactory__).

____
*/
